  * log38 and log39 came back byte-identical to log37 - not even the 1 s flush
  * cadence wrote anything - so the 0100 kernel stopped before the release, at
  * the new A6XX_GBIF_HALT access. GBIF is the GPU's bus interface in the CX
- * power domain, and this probe never actually brings CX up (the GDSC is only
- * read, the CX rail votes time out), so touching it can hang the whole
- * interconnect. 0101 keeps that access and the gmu_clk_src write behind
+ * power domain; the CX GDSC does come up in this probe, but the CX rail itself
+ * is never voted (only gfx.lvl is raised), so touching GBIF can still hang the
+ * whole interconnect. 0101 keeps that access and the gmu_clk_src write behind
  * gpu_gbif=1 / gpu_gmuclk=1, and gives every post-release GMU read its own
  * print, so the next log names the register that stalls instead of the phase.
+ * log42 showed the blind spot rather than new hardware state: the ring still
+ * held the previous gpu=0 boot's 24 minute SpringBoard log, so a kernel that
+ * dies in under a second leaves nothing saying whether it even started. 0102
+ * makes the log self-identifying (sweet_ufs.c writes once before its first
+ * sleep and can be paced at hz/10 with sweetlogfast=1) and makes this bring-up
+ * print, pause, then touch the risky register, so the last durable line names
+ * the access that wedged the machine.
  */
 #if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_SWEET)
 #include <sys/param.h>
